    <h2>One-paragraph bio, for quotation</h2>
    <p style="background:var(--bg-alt);padding:1.5rem 1.75rem;border-left:3px solid var(--gold-500);border-radius:0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0;margin:1rem 0 2rem;font-size:0.98rem;line-height:1.7">
      Pikes Peak Web Designs builds custom-coded websites for small service businesses across the U.S. and Canada. Jon Ajinga founded it in Colorado Springs, and he writes every site from scratch instead of filling in a template. His sites score 95 to 100 on Google's speed test, meet WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility, and are built to show up in Google's map results (the "local pack"). Clients pay a flat $175 a month with $0 down, instead of a $5,000 design deposit. The service agreement and the Owner's Guide are public, so owners can judge the work before any sales conversation.
    </p>

    <h2>Two-line bio, for short attribution</h2>
    <p style="background:var(--bg-alt);padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-left:3px solid var(--gold-500);border-radius:0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0;margin:1rem 0 2rem;font-size:0.98rem;line-height:1.7">
      Jon Ajinga is the founder of <a href="https://pikespeakwebdesigns.com" rel="noopener" class="inline-link">Pikes Peak Web Designs</a>, a Colorado Springs business that builds custom-coded websites for small service businesses across the U.S. and Canada. He writes about local search, page speed, and the hidden cost of "good enough" templates for the trades.
    </p>

    <h2>Topics I speak on</h2>
    <p>I'll go on the record about any of these, in writing, on a call, or as a podcast guest.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Local search for service businesses.</strong> Google's map results (the "local pack"), Google Business Profile, structured data, the penalty for copy-and-paste location pages (doorway pages), consistent name, address, and phone details (NAP), and why "near me" searches reward specific pages.</li>
      <li><strong>What page speed is worth.</strong> What a slow site costs in lost mobile visitors, and how each extra second of loading cuts the calls and bookings.</li>
      <li><strong>The "good enough" website trap.</strong> Why generic, machine-written copy ranks worse over time, and why a page can look finished and still not bring in calls.</li>
      <li><strong>Website security for non-technical owners.</strong> Why a service-business site doesn't need a content management system, what maintenance retainers cost, and the simpler, custom-coded alternative.</li>
      <li><strong>The Service Site Standard.</strong> The seven principles behind every site I build: one owner from start to finish, custom code over off-the-shelf systems, posted prices and process, speed built in from the start, accessibility by default, local before global, and proven, dependable tools. <a href="/method/" class="inline-link">Read the principles</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Posted prices.</strong> Why posted prices bring more qualified inquiries, why "request a quote" buttons cost more business than they protect, and how a flat monthly price changes the relationship with a web designer.</li>
      <li><strong>The view from each trade.</strong> Roofing's storm season, HVAC's seasonal demand, plumbing's emergency-call pages, and what each trade needs from its website.</li>
    </ul>

